Reflection:-

Q) What effect did changing the learning rate

Changing the learning rate (α) affected how fast and how stable the agent learned. 
A high learning rate made learning faster but unstable, causing large fluctuations in Q-values. 
A low learning rate made learning slower but more stable and consistent.

Q) What effect did changing the discount factor

Changing the discount factor (γ) affected how much the agent valued future rewards.
A low discount factor made the agent ignore the final goal reward, so it failed to learn properly.
A high discount factor helped the agent focus on long-term rewards,
allowing it to learn the correct path to the goal.


Q) At what point in training did the agent start meaningfully improving and why you think that is.

In the beginning, most episodes ended randomly in holes, so the Q-table stayed close to zero and
there was no clear learning. Once the agent randomly reached the goal a few times,
it finally received the reward signal (1).
This reward then began to propagate backward through the Q-values, 
helping the agent recognize which actions were better.

After this point, the agent slowly shifted from mostly random exploration to choosing better 
actions more often. As more successful episodes occurred, the policy became more stable, 
and the success rate started increasing consistently